# This repo is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, so text files arrive in the
# working tree with CRLF. That is harmless for Rust and for PowerShell, but not for files a
# Linux tool reads as shell: snapcraft.yaml embeds a shell fragment in `override-pull`, and a
# trailing CR turns the last argument of every line into something with a carriage return in
# it. Building the snap from a Windows checkout through WSL hits this immediately.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.desktop       text eol=lf

# PowerShell keeps CRLF: the installer scripts are only ever run on Windows.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Belt and braces for the icons. Git detects these as binary on its own, but an explicit
# marker means no future .gitattributes rule can accidentally mangle them.
*.png           binary
*.ico           binary
*.icns          binary
